From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Max Schettler <max@simplyblock.io>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 16:30:00 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] nfsd: send CB_OFFLOAD with the async copy's own callback
 stateid

DROP THIS PATCH WHEN THE KERNEL MOVES TO 6.18.58 OR LATER. Stable carries
the identical two-line fix from 6.18.58 on, as commit 22732b0f290a ("nfsd:
fix all-zero stateid in CB_OFFLOAD for async COPY"), and this patch will
then fail to apply. We are on 6.18.55; see linux.hash and the
BR2_LINUX_KERNEL_CUSTOM_VERSION_VALUE in the pnfs_qemu_* defconfigs.

An asynchronous COPY answers the client immediately with a callback stateid
and reports completion later in CB_OFFLOAD. The client registers its waiter
under the stateid from the reply and matches the callback against it by
memcmp (nfs4_callback_offload). nfsd sends a zeroed stateid instead, so the
match never succeeds.

nfsd4_copy() calls dup_copy_fields() before nfs4_init_copy_state(), so the
async_copy it hands to the background thread carries a copy of cp_res taken
while cb_stateid was still zero. The memcpy that follows writes the real
stateid only into result->cb_stateid, which is &copy->cp_res of the
transient per-request structure and reaches the client in the COPY reply.
async_copy->cp_res.cb_stateid stays zero, and that is the one that matters:
nfsd4_send_cb_offload() does

	memcpy(&cbo->co_res, &copy->cp_res, sizeof(copy->cp_res));

from the async_copy, and encode_cb_offload4args() puts cbo->co_res.cb_stateid
on the wire.

The ordering is deliberate. The 6.18.y backport 9b4e5e9ba5ae ("nfsd: fix
stale s2s_cp_stateids IDR entry for async COPY", mainline d0beaee498e1)
moved dup_copy_fields() ahead of nfs4_init_copy_state() so the
s2s_cp_stateids IDR is registered against the durable async_copy rather
than into per-rqstp COMPOUND memory that the next request reuses. Moving it
back is not an option either, because dup_copy_fields() also copies
cp_stateid and would overwrite the stateid the backport moved to the async
copy. Populate async_copy->cp_res.cb_stateid once the stateid exists
instead.

Observed on a pNFS mount as an S3 CompleteMultipartUpload that never
returned. The gateway's join of the uploaded parts is a copy_file_range
against the destination object, which nfsd serves as an asynchronous COPY.
Client and server tracepoints for one such copy, same destination file
handle 0x03371d99 on both sides:

  nfs4_copy:       len=8388608 sync=0 cb_stateid=1:0xd062d9e9
  nfs4_cb_offload: fhandle=0x03371d99 cb_stateid=0:0x7bd5c66f
  nfsd_cb_offload: fh_hash=0x03371d99 stateid 00000000:00000000 count=8388608 status=0

The copy itself succeeds and CB_OFFLOAD is delivered and acknowledged;
nfs4_callback_offload() simply cannot match it, parks it on
pending_cb_stateids and returns NFS4_OK, and the waiting task is never
completed. A client whose handle_async_copy() has the
wait_for_completion_interruptible_timeout() and OFFLOAD_STATUS poll recovers
after a delay; one that waits on the completion alone waits forever.

Mainline never needs this: the async COPY rework, f307b4f7ed17 ("nfsd:
split nfsd4_copy into transient and durable async copy objects"), allocates
the stateid up front, writes result->cb_stateid and only then duplicates,
and warns in a comment that duplicating first would leave the callback
stateid zeroed. That rework was judged too risky to backport, which is why
6.18.y took the targeted fix instead.

Fixes: 9b4e5e9ba5ae ("nfsd: fix stale s2s_cp_stateids IDR entry for async COPY")
Signed-off-by: Max Schettler <max@simplyblock.io>
---
 fs/nfsd/nfs4proc.c | 11 +++++++++++
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+)

diff --git a/fs/nfsd/nfs4proc.c b/fs/nfsd/nfs4proc.c
index bc14c39..a1f20e5 100644
--- a/fs/nfsd/nfs4proc.c
+++ b/fs/nfsd/nfs4proc.c
@@ -2084,6 +2084,17 @@
 			goto out_dec_async_copy_err;
 		memcpy(&result->cb_stateid, &async_copy->cp_stateid.cs_stid,
 			sizeof(result->cb_stateid));
+		/*
+		 * CB_OFFLOAD encodes cp_cb_offload.co_res.cb_stateid, which
+		 * nfsd4_send_cb_offload() takes from this async_copy's
+		 * cp_res. dup_copy_fields() ran before nfs4_init_copy_state()
+		 * created the stateid, so that cp_res still holds zeros and
+		 * only the reply's copy got the real one. Reordering the dup
+		 * is not an option: it also copies cp_stateid, and would
+		 * overwrite the stateid created just above.
+		 */
+		memcpy(&async_copy->cp_res.cb_stateid, &async_copy->cp_stateid.cs_stid,
+		       sizeof(async_copy->cp_res.cb_stateid));
 		if ((READ_ONCE(copy->nf_dst->nf_file->f_mode) &
 			       FMODE_NOCMTIME) != 0)
 			async_copy->attr_update = true;
-- 
2.56.0

